VMSS - Virtual machine scale set is  Microsoft Azure compute service that lets you 
deploy, manage, and automatically scale a group of load-balanced virtual machines (VMs). 
Instead of manually creating and configuring VMs one by one, you manage them as a group based on traffic demand or a set schedule.

Autoscaling: Automatically adds (scales out) or removes (scales in) VM instances based on metrics like CPU usage or on a defined schedule.
Load Balancing: Integrates with Azure Load Balancer and Azure Application Gateway to distribute traffic across healthy instances
High Availability: Spreads instances across availability zones and fault domains to keep apps running if a component fails.

same Auto scaling groups in AWS

How does it work?
For example, suppose we have an application running on virtual machines.
Initially, we have 2 VMs.
During normal traffic, 2 VMs are sufficient.
During peak traffic, CPU utilization increases.
VMSS automatically scales out and creates additional VMs.
When traffic decreases, it scales in and removes unnecessary VMs.

What do we manage?
Operating system
VM configuration
Software installation
Runtime and application deployment
Patching and updates
Networking and security
Scaling configuration
Real-time example: An e-commerce application running on multiple VMs. During a festival sale, traffic increases, so VMSS automatically increases the VM count to handle additional requests.

